{"final":false,"fragments":[[8,{"precedence":20,"provenance":{"tool_names":["build","edit_file","grep","judge","list_dir","read_file","write_file"]},"source":"tools_suite","text":"  read_file(path, offset=1, limit) -> line-numbered text ('<n>\\t<line>'); offset is a 1-based line. Output is capped at ~12 KB inline; if the file is larger, the tail is a marker '… <N> more line(s); read_file(path, <next>) for the rest' — call it with that <next> offset to page.\n  list_dir(path) -> directory entries\n  grep(regex, path='.') -> matching 'file:line: text' (a real regex, not a substring)\n  edit_file(path, search, replace, replace_all=false) -> surgical search/replace; `search` must match the file's exact bytes EXACTLY ONE place or it errors (add context to disambiguate, or pass replace_all=true to change every occurrence, e.g. a rename). Match file content only — do NOT include read_file's leading '<n>\\t' line-number prefix; keep `search` minimal (1-3 lines, just enough to be unique) (SIDE EFFECT)\n  write_file(path, text) -> create/overwrite a file (SIDE EFFECT)\n  build() -> compiles environment.c into the guest runtime: 'built' or the compiler's errors\n  judge() -> runs the probe over environment.c in w2m: the rows that differ from Windows's, or the item runs' verdicts once all are equal"}]],"history_ref":{"dropped":null,"from_seq":11,"to_seq":141,"tokens_before":10510},"results":[{"call_id":"c0","error":"","ok":true,"output":"1\t# Task: write environment.c for w2m\n2\t\n3\tw2m runs Windows x64 programs on macOS. It translates the program's x86-64 code and runs it inside\n4\ta virtual machine on Apple silicon. The program's kernel32 imports are served by a \"guest\n5\truntime\": freestanding C compiled for AArch64 with clang --target=arm64-apple-macos14 -O2 -std=c11 -Wall -Werror -ffreestanding -fno-builtin -fno-stack-protector -fno-exceptions -fno-unwind-tables -fno-asynchronous-unwind-tables -mgeneral-regs-only, linked into the VM. There is no\n6\tC library: no memcpy, no memset, no headers but \"runtime.h\" (read it). Arguments arrive per the\n7\tMicrosoft x64 calling convention: the first four in the C parameters, and a fifth parameter `rsp`,\n8\twhen declared, is the guest stack pointer at the call (the fifth argument at *(u64 *)(rsp + 0x28)).\n9\tGuest pointers can be dereferenced directly. Each function's return value goes to RAX.\n10\t\n11\tWrite `environment.c` in this directory, defining exactly these functions:\n12\t\n13\t  u64 k32_GetEnvironmentStringsW(void);\n14\t  u64 k32_FreeEnvironmentStringsW(u64 block);\n15\t  u64 k32_lstrlenW(u64 string);\n16\t\n17\tThe process's environment block is the host's: UTF-16 entries, each ending in a NUL, then one more NUL,\n18\tat the address W2M_ENVIRONMENT_VA (an empty block is a single NUL there). runtime.h declares\n19\t  u64 w2m_heap_take(u64 size);          a process-heap block of exactly `size` bytes (HeapSize reports it),\n20\t                                        or 0 when the arena is full\n21\t  void w2m_heap_release(u64 api, u64 p); gives a block back\n22\tProbes see the process heap through GetProcessHeap and HeapSize, which use the same blocks.\n23\t\n24\tThe last-error code is w2m_teb()->last_error_value. Return from each k32_ function through\n25\tw2m_logged(W2M_API_<name>, arg0, return_value), which records the call and returns its third\n26\targument. Call w2m_unimplemented(W2M_API_<name>, detail) for an argument form you do not support;\n27\tit stops the run.\n28\t\n29\tThe judge runs the probe (probe_environment.c, compiled by Windows's clang) in w2m over your file, once per\n30\tcontract: contract-environment_set.json (environment block ['ZED=last', 'alpha=1', '=C:=C:\\\\w2m', 'SteamAppId=480', 'Path=C:\\\\Windows\\\\system32'], command line ending 'child-set'); contract-environment_empty.json (environment block [], command line ending 'child-empty'). Each contract's rows are what Windows printed on the runners; the probe's\n31\toutput in w2m must equal the